---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="How ShouldISpend Calculates Spending Verdicts"
  description="Learn how ShouldISpend evaluates major spending decisions using income, savings, debt, emergency cushion, risk, and personal flexibility."
>
  <main class="methodology-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How ShouldISpend Calculates Spending Verdicts</h1>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend is built to give financial context before major purchases. The calculators do not replace financial advice, but they help users slow down, compare tradeoffs, and understand whether a spending decision looks comfortable, aggressive, or risky.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The Core Idea</h2>
      <p>
        Most spending decisions depend on more than the price tag. A $5,000 vacation, $700 car payment, or $15,000 wedding may be manageable for one household and dangerous for another. ShouldISpend evaluates the decision against the user’s broader financial picture.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What the Calculators Consider</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Income:</strong> whether the cost fits reasonably within household earnings.</li>
        <li><strong>Savings:</strong> whether the purchase would drain available cash or emergency reserves.</li>
        <li><strong>Debt:</strong> whether existing obligations already create financial pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> whether normal bills and future goals remain manageable afterward.</li>
        <li><strong>Risk:</strong> whether the decision could create stress if income changes or surprise costs appear.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Verdicts Are Conservative</h2>
      <p>
        ShouldISpend generally favors financial breathing room. Major purchases can be worth it, but they become riskier when they depend on credit card debt, thin savings, unstable income, or ignored future expenses.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Educational, Not Financial Advice</h2>
      <p>
        These tools provide educational estimates only. They are not personalized financial, legal, tax, or investment advice. Users should consider their full situation and consult a qualified professional when needed.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>

  <style>
    .methodology-page {
      max-width: 820px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 72px 24px;
    }

    .hero {
      margin-bottom: 48px;
      text-align: center;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: #0f766e;
      font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
      line-height: 1;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
      color: #061126;
    }

    h2 {
      margin-top: 44px;
      margin-bottom: 14px;
      color: #061126;
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: #475569;
      line-height: 1.8;
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    li {
      margin-bottom: 12px;
    }
  </style>
</Layout>